window.CSE_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"gi-constitution-01","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"According to the Declaration of Principles in the 1987 Constitution, in whom does sovereignty reside?","options":["The President","The Congress","The people","The Supreme Court"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Constitution states that sovereignty resides in the people and that all government authority emanates from them. The three branches only exercise authority that comes from the people.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. II, Sec. 1"},{"id":"gi-constitution-02","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What is the minimum age of a Senator on the day of the election?","options":["Twenty-five years","Thirty-five years","Thirty years","Forty years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Senator must be at least thirty-five years old on the day of the election. Twenty-five is the minimum for a Member of the House, and forty is the minimum for the President.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VI, Secs. 3 and 6; Art. VII, Sec. 2"},{"id":"gi-constitution-03","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Aside from being at least eighteen and not disqualified by law, what residency must a citizen satisfy in order to vote?","options":["Six months in the Philippines and one year in the place of voting","Two years in the Philippines and one year in the place of voting","One year in the Philippines and three months in the place of voting","One year in the Philippines and six months in the place of voting"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Suffrage requires residence in the Philippines for at least one year and in the place of voting for at least six months immediately before the election. The swapped pairing is the usual trap.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. V, Sec. 1"},{"id":"gi-constitution-04","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Lorna was born in 1968 to a Filipino mother and a foreign father, and she elected Philippine citizenship upon reaching the age of majority. What is her status?","options":["A natural-born citizen of the Philippines","A naturalized citizen of the Philippines","A citizen who is not considered natural-born","A foreign national until she takes an oath"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Those born before January 17, 1973 of Filipino mothers who elect Philippine citizenship at majority are expressly deemed natural-born. The act of electing does not reduce her status, even though natural-born citizens otherwise need no act to perfect citizenship.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. IV, Sec. 1(3) and Sec. 2"},{"id":"gi-constitution-05","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What share of the total number of representatives, including those under the party list, do party-list representatives constitute?","options":["Ten per centum","Twenty per centum","Twenty-five per centum","Thirty per centum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Constitution provides that party-list representatives shall constitute twenty per centum of the total number of representatives, a total that includes the party-list seats themselves.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VI, Sec. 5(2)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-06","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which of these bills must originate exclusively in the House of Representatives?","options":["A bill amending the Family Code","A bill defining a new criminal offense","A bill reorganizing the national police","A bill increasing the public debt"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bills authorizing an increase of the public debt are on the constitutional list, together with appropriation, revenue or tariff bills, bills of local application and private bills. The Senate may still propose or concur with amendments to them.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VI, Sec. 24"},{"id":"gi-constitution-07","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What is the national language of the Philippines under the 1987 Constitution?","options":["Tagalog","Pilipino","Filipino","English"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Constitution names Filipino as the national language, to be developed and enriched on the basis of existing Philippine and other languages. English is an official language until otherwise provided by law, but not the national language.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. XIV, Secs. 6 and 7"},{"id":"gi-constitution-08","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"A Vice-President succeeds a President who dies in office and then serves as President for five years of the unexpired term. May this person run for President in the next regular election?","options":["Yes, because succession is not the same as being elected President","No, because a successor who served more than four years is barred from election to that office","Yes, but only for one more term and only if the Congress consents","No, because anyone who succeeds as President is barred whatever the length of service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bar applies to a person who has succeeded as President and served as such for more than four years, and five years passes that line. The rule that every successor is barred regardless of length overstates it, because a successor who served four years or less is not covered.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VII, Sec. 4"},{"id":"gi-constitution-09","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What are the term of office of a Senator and the limit on consecutive terms?","options":["Three years, with no more than three consecutive terms","Four years, with no more than two consecutive terms","Six years, with no more than three consecutive terms","Six years, with no more than two consecutive terms"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Senators serve for six years and may not serve more than two consecutive terms. Three years with three consecutive terms is the rule for Members of the House.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VI, Secs. 4 and 7"},{"id":"gi-constitution-10","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Which statement about a state of martial law is correct under the 1987 Constitution?","options":["It does not automatically suspend the privilege of the writ of habeas corpus","It gives military courts jurisdiction over civilians even where civil courts function","It supplants the legislative assemblies until the proclamation is lifted","It suspends the operation of the Constitution in the area it covers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Constitution says martial law does not suspend the Constitution, supplant civil courts or legislative assemblies, give military courts jurisdiction over civilians where civil courts can function, or automatically suspend the privilege of the writ. Suspending the privilege requires its own decision by the President.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VII, Sec. 18, par. 4"},{"id":"gi-constitution-11","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What concurrence makes a treaty or international agreement valid and effective?","options":["A majority of all the Members of the Senate","Two-thirds of all the Members of the Senate","Two-thirds of both Houses voting separately","Three-fourths of all the Members of the Congress"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A treaty needs the concurrence of at least two-thirds of all the Members of the Senate, and the House has no vote on it. Two-thirds of both Houses voting separately is the rule for declaring a state of war.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VII, Sec. 21; Art. VI, Sec. 23(1)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-12","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"How is the Supreme Court composed under the 1987 Constitution?","options":["A Chief Justice and eight Associate Justices","A Chief Justice and ten Associate Justices","A Chief Justice and twelve Associate Justices","A Chief Justice and fourteen Associate Justices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Supreme Court has a Chief Justice and fourteen Associate Justices, or fifteen Members in all. It may sit en banc or in divisions of three, five or seven Members.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VIII, Sec. 4(1)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-13","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Who is the ex officio Chairman of the Judicial and Bar Council?","options":["The Chief Justice","The Secretary of Justice","The President of the Integrated Bar","The Clerk of the Supreme Court"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Chief Justice is the ex officio Chairman. The Secretary of Justice is an ex officio Member, and the Clerk of the Supreme Court is the ex officio Secretary of the Council.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. VIII, Sec. 8(1) and (3)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-14","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Civil service appointments are generally determined by competitive examination. Which positions are excepted from this rule?","options":["Entry-level, temporary, casual, or other non-permanent positions","Clerical, administrative, supervisory, or other support positions","Policy-determining, primarily confidential, or highly technical positions","Regional, provincial, municipal, or other local government positions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Merit and fitness are determined by competitive examination as far as practicable, except for positions that are policy-determining, primarily confidential, or highly technical. The other groupings do not appear in the constitutional exception.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. IX-B, Sec. 2(2)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-15","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which of these is NOT one of the Constitutional Commissions named in Article IX?","options":["The Civil Service Commission","The Commission on Elections","The Commission on Audit","The Commission on Human Rights"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Article IX names only the Civil Service Commission, the Commission on Elections and the Commission on Audit. The Commission on Human Rights is an independent office created separately under Article XIII.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. IX-A, Sec. 1; Art. XIII, Sec. 17(1)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-16","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A candidate loses a senatorial race. Eight months later, the President wishes to appoint this person to head a government-owned corporation. Is the appointment allowed?","options":["Yes, because government-owned corporations are outside the prohibition","Yes, provided the Commission on Appointments gives its consent","No, because one year has not yet passed since the election","No, because losing candidates are permanently barred from office"],"answer":2,"explanation":"No candidate who lost in any election may be appointed within one year after that election to any government office or government-owned or controlled corporation. The bar is limited to one year, so it is not permanent, and corporations are expressly covered.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. IX-B, Sec. 6"},{"id":"gi-constitution-17","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A city mayor voluntarily resigns midway through a third consecutive term. Does the resignation allow the mayor to seek the same office in the next election?","options":["Yes, because the third term was not served in full","No, because voluntary renunciation does not interrupt continuity of service","Yes, because a resignation automatically resets the count of terms","No, because mayors are limited to two consecutive terms in office"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Elective local officials other than barangay officials are limited to three consecutive terms, and voluntary renunciation for any length of time is not an interruption of service. Resigning therefore does not avoid the limit.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. X, Sec. 8"},{"id":"gi-constitution-18","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which of these officials may be removed from office by impeachment?","options":["A Senator of the Philippines","A Secretary of a Cabinet department","A judge of a Regional Trial Court","A Member of the Civil Service Commission"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Impeachment covers the President, the Vice-President, Members of the Supreme Court, Members of the Constitutional Commissions and the Ombudsman. All other public officers may be removed as provided by law, but not by impeachment.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. XI, Sec. 2"},{"id":"gi-constitution-19","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"What votes are needed for the House to affirm a favorable impeachment resolution and for the Senate to convict?","options":["One-third of all House Members; two-thirds of all Senators","Two-thirds of all House Members; two-thirds of all Senators","A majority of all House Members; three-fourths of all Senators","One-third of all House Members; a majority of all Senators"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At least one-third of all House Members can affirm the resolution, or override a contrary one, while conviction needs the concurrence of two-thirds of all Senators. The low House threshold is the commonly missed part.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. XI, Sec. 3(3) and (6)"},{"id":"gi-constitution-20","subject":"general","skill":"constitution","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"What support does a people's initiative petition need in order to propose amendments directly to the Constitution?","options":["Ten percent of all registered voters, with three percent in every legislative district","Twelve percent of all registered voters, with five percent in every legislative district","Three percent of all registered voters, with twelve percent in every legislative district","Twelve percent of all registered voters, with three percent in every legislative district"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The petition needs at least twelve per centum of the total registered voters, with every legislative district represented by at least three per centum of its registered voters. Initiative may also be used no oftener than once every five years.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. XVII, Sec. 2"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-01","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Under R.A. 6713, who make up the \"family\" of a public official or employee?","options":["Spouse and all children regardless of age or civil status","Spouse, parents, and all brothers and sisters who are minors","Spouse and unmarried children under eighteen years of age","All relatives within the fourth civil degree of the official"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Act defines family as spouses and unmarried children under eighteen years of age. The fourth civil degree belongs to the separate definition of relatives.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 3(g)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-02","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Within how many working days from receipt must public officials and employees respond to letters sent by the public?","options":["Five working days","Ten working days","Fifteen working days","Thirty working days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The duty to act promptly requires a response within fifteen working days from receipt, and the reply must state the action taken on the request.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 5(a)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-03","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"A municipal clerk serves all applicants without unfair discrimination and regardless of their party preference. Which norm of conduct does this best show?","options":["Political neutrality","Simple living","Professionalism","Nationalism and patriotism"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Political neutrality requires service to everyone without unfair discrimination and regardless of party affiliation or preference. The other norms deal with modest lifestyles, excellence in duties, and loyalty to the Republic.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 4(A)(d)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-04","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"An official actively promotes the use of locally produced goods, resources and technology and encourages pride of country. Which norm of conduct is being observed?","options":["Commitment to democracy","Nationalism and patriotism","Responsiveness to the public","Justness and sincerity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nationalism and patriotism expressly includes promoting locally produced goods, resources and technology and encouraging appreciation and pride of country and people.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 4(A)(f)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-05","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"As far as practicable, official papers and documents must contain not more than how many signatories?","options":["Two","Five","Seven","Three"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Official papers must be processed within a reasonable time and contain, as far as practicable, not more than three signatories. If an authorized signatory is absent, the next-in-rank or officer in charge signs.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 5(c)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-06","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Up to what civil degree of consanguinity or affinity does the term \"relatives\" extend under R.A. 6713?","options":["Second civil degree","Fourth civil degree","Third civil degree","Sixth civil degree"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Relatives are all persons related to the official or employee within the fourth civil degree of consanguinity or affinity, including bilas, inso and balae.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 3(k)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-07","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which of these from a foreign government may a public official accept under the consent given by the Congress in R.A. 6713?","options":["A luxury watch given as a personal present","A cash gift offered after a trade negotiation","A new vehicle for the official's private use","A scholarship or fellowship grant for study"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Congress consents to scholarship or fellowship grants, medical treatment, gifts of nominal value given as a souvenir or mark of courtesy, and certain travel grants outside the Philippines. A luxury watch, cash or a vehicle does not fit any of these.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 7(d)(ii)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-08","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"After the filing that follows assumption of office, by what date each year must the Statement of Assets, Liabilities and Net Worth be filed?","options":["On or before January 15","On or before March 31","On or before April 30","On or before June 30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The statements are filed within thirty days after assumption of office, on or before April 30 of every year thereafter, and within thirty days after separation from the service.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 8(A)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-09","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"When a conflict of interest arises, what are the periods to resign from a private enterprise and to divest shareholdings, counted from assumption of office?","options":["Resign in 30 days and divest in 60 days","Resign in 60 days and divest in 30 days","Resign in 15 days and divest in 30 days","Resign in 30 days and divest in 90 days"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The official must resign from any private business position within thirty days from assumption of office and divest shareholdings or interest within sixty days from that assumption.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 9"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-10","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Six months after resigning, a former bureau director is offered a consultancy by a private firm that the bureau licenses. How does R.A. 6713 treat the offer?","options":["Allowed, because the prohibition ends upon resignation from office","Allowed, provided the firm's board publicly discloses the engagement","Prohibited, because the ban lasts for one year after separation","Prohibited, because the ban lasts for five years after separation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Consultancy in an enterprise regulated, supervised or licensed by the official's office is barred during incumbency, and the prohibition continues for one year after resignation, retirement or separation. Six months falls inside that period.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 7(b)(1) and last paragraph"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-11","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"An employee's violation of R.A. 6713 is proven in a proper administrative proceeding, but no criminal case has been filed. What is the effect?","options":["Removal must wait until a court issues a conviction","The proven violation is sufficient cause for removal or dismissal","Only a fine may be imposed while no criminal case is pending","The administrative finding lapses unless a criminal case follows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Any violation proven in a proper administrative proceeding is sufficient cause for removal or dismissal even if no criminal prosecution is instituted. Administrative and criminal liability do not depend on each other here.","source":"R.A. 6713, Sec. 11(b)"},{"id":"gi-ra6713-12","subject":"general","skill":"ra6713","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which body has the primary responsibility for the administration and enforcement of R.A. 6713?","options":["The Civil Service Commission","The Office of the Ombudsman","The Commission on Audit","The Department of Justice"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Civil Service Commission has the primary responsibility and is also authorized to issue the implementing rules. The Ombudsman has specific roles, such as prescribing gift regulations and protecting citizens who denounce violations, but not this primary responsibility.","source":"R.A. 6713, Secs. 7(d) and 12"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-01","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Where and in what year did the United Nations General Assembly proclaim the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?","options":["San Francisco, in 1945","Paris, in 1948","Geneva, in 1949","New York, in 1966"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The General Assembly proclaimed the Declaration in Paris on 10 December 1948, through resolution 217 A. The year 1966 belongs to the two International Covenants, not to the Declaration.","source":"Universal Declaration of Human Rights, UN introductory text; G.A. Res. 217 A, 10 December 1948"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-02","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What does the Philippines renounce as an instrument of national policy under the 1987 Constitution?","options":["Foreign trade","Treaties","War","Foreign aid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The State renounces war as an instrument of national policy and adheres to a policy of peace, equality, justice, freedom, cooperation and amity with all nations.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. II, Sec. 2"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-03","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Unless majority is attained earlier under the applicable law, a child under the Convention on the Rights of the Child is every human being below what age?","options":["Fifteen years","Sixteen years","Eighteen years","Twenty-one years"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Convention defines a child as every human being below the age of eighteen years, unless majority is attained earlier under the law applicable to the child.","source":"Convention on the Rights of the Child, Art. 1"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-04","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Among these instruments, which states that primary education shall be compulsory and available free to all?","options":["The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights","The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights","The Convention Against Torture","The Charter of the United Nations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Education is treated as an economic, social and cultural right, and the Covenant on that subject requires compulsory and free primary education. The civil and political rights Covenant does not contain this clause.","source":"International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, Art. 13(2)(a)"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-05","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"How does the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights describe the general obligation of a State Party?","options":["To guarantee every right in full at once, whatever its resources","To leave the rights to the private sector and to civil society","To work progressively toward the rights, using maximum available resources","To apply the rights only after a request from the United Nations"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each State Party undertakes to take steps to the maximum of its available resources, with a view to achieving progressively the full realization of the rights. Progressive realization does not mean the State may do nothing.","source":"International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, Art. 2(1)"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-06","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"During an officially proclaimed public emergency, a State Party may not derogate from which of these rights under the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights?","options":["Liberty of movement","Peaceful assembly","Freedom of expression","Freedom from torture"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Covenant allows no derogation from a listed set of articles that includes Article 7 on torture. The articles on movement, expression and assembly are outside that list, so they may be limited in a genuine emergency to the extent strictly required.","source":"International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, Art. 4(2), with Arts. 7, 12, 19 and 21"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-07","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A commander argues that a threat of war justified the torture of a detainee. How does the Anti-Torture Act of 2009 treat this defense?","options":["It may be accepted if a superior officer approved it in writing","It can never be invoked as a justification for torture","It reduces the penalty but does not remove criminal liability","It may be accepted only during a proclaimed state of martial law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Act treats freedom from torture as an absolute right. A state of war, a threat of war, internal political instability, or any other public emergency can never be invoked as a justification.","source":"R.A. 9745, Sec. 6"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-08","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Under the Anti-Torture Act of 2009, when may a confession obtained through torture be used as evidence?","options":["When used against a person accused of committing the torture","When the confessant later affirms it before a judge in open court","When other independent evidence corroborates the confession","When the case involves a crime committed against national security"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A statement obtained through torture is inadmissible in any proceeding, with one exception: it may be used against persons accused of committing torture. Later affirmation or corroboration does not cure it under this rule.","source":"R.A. 9745, Sec. 8"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-09","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A soldier receives an \"Order of Battle\" directing an act of enforced disappearance. What does R.A. 10353 say about the order?","options":["It is a justifying circumstance when issued by a superior officer","It is valid only if the President confirms it within three days","It is unlawful, and the person receiving it has the right to disobey","It is a mitigating circumstance that reduces the soldier's liability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An Order of Battle or similar order causing enforced or involuntary disappearance is unlawful and cannot be invoked as a justifying or exempting circumstance. The person receiving it has the right to disobey.","source":"R.A. 10353, Sec. 5"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-10","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What did the Supreme Court hold about the power of the Commission on Human Rights over alleged civil and political rights violations?","options":["It may try and decide such cases with finality, as a court does","It may investigate and find facts, but it cannot adjudicate cases","It may review and reverse decisions of the regular trial courts","It may act only after a formal referral from the Department of Justice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Constitution empowers the Commission to investigate on its own or on complaint, and the Court ruled that to investigate is not to adjudicate. The Commission therefore cannot try and decide cases as courts or quasi-judicial bodies do.","source":"1987 Constitution, Art. XIII, Sec. 18(1); Cariño v. Commission on Human Rights, G.R. No. 96681, December 2, 1991"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-11","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Under the Charter of the United Nations, what must parties to a dispute likely to endanger international peace do first of all?","options":["Refer the matter directly to the Security Council for sanctions","Seek a solution through negotiation, mediation or other peaceful means","Exercise their inherent right of individual or collective self-defense","Suspend diplomatic and economic relations with the other party"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Parties shall first of all seek a solution by negotiation, enquiry, mediation, conciliation, arbitration, judicial settlement, regional arrangements or other peaceful means of their own choice. Self-defense applies if an armed attack occurs, not as a first response to a dispute.","source":"Charter of the United Nations, Art. 33(1); Art. 51"},{"id":"gi-peace-rights-12","subject":"general","skill":"peace-rights","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Executive Order No. 3, s. 2001, since repealed, set out the Six Paths to Peace. Under which path did peace advocacy and peace education fall?","options":["Pursuit of social, economic and political reforms","Peaceful, negotiated settlement with the different rebel groups","Programs for reconciliation, reintegration and rehabilitation","Building and nurturing a climate conducive to peace"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Building and nurturing a climate conducive to peace included peace advocacy, peace education and confidence-building measures. Negotiated settlement instead covered face-to-face talks with rebel groups and implementing the resulting agreements. Executive Order No. 158, s. 2021, repealed this order, and its current framework places peace advocacy and peace education under the objective of enhancing resilience for peace.","source":"Executive Order No. 3, s. 2001, Sec. 4(f); Executive Order No. 158, s. 2021, Secs. 1(b)(ii) and 6"},{"id":"gi-environment-01","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which law is known as the Ecological Solid Waste Management Act of 2000?","options":["Republic Act No. 8749","Republic Act No. 9275","Republic Act No. 7586","Republic Act No. 9003"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Republic Act No. 9003 carries this title. Republic Act No. 8749 is the Clean Air Act, No. 9275 is the Clean Water Act, and No. 7586 is the NIPAS Act, now amended by the Expanded NIPAS Act of 2018 (R.A. 11038).","source":"R.A. 9003, Sec. 1; R.A. 8749, Sec. 1; R.A. 9275, Sec. 1; R.A. 7586, Sec. 1; R.A. 11038, Sec. 1"},{"id":"gi-environment-02","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Under P.D. 1586, what must be secured before undertaking or operating a project or area declared environmentally critical?","options":["A Certificate of Ancestral Domain Title","An Environmental Compliance Certificate","A Materials Recovery Facility Clearance","An Environmental Sanitation Clearance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No person, partnership or corporation may undertake or operate a declared environmentally critical project or area without first securing an Environmental Compliance Certificate.","source":"P.D. 1586, Sec. 4"},{"id":"gi-environment-03","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which of these household actions is a prohibited act under R.A. 9003?","options":["Open burning of solid waste in the backyard","Composting yard waste in a backyard bin","Sorting recyclables into separate containers","Bringing sorted bottles to a recovery facility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The open burning of solid waste is expressly prohibited. Composting, sorting and bringing recyclables to a recovery facility are the practices the Act encourages.","source":"R.A. 9003, Sec. 48(3)"},{"id":"gi-environment-04","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Under R.A. 9003, where shall the segregation of wastes primarily be conducted?","options":["At the sanitary landfill","At the source of the waste","At the city transfer station","Inside the collection truck"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Segregation shall primarily be conducted at the source, including household, institutional, industrial, commercial and agricultural sources. Sorting only at later stages misses the point of this requirement.","source":"R.A. 9003, Sec. 21"},{"id":"gi-environment-05","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which of these is expressly exempted from the incineration ban in the Philippine Clean Air Act of 1999?","options":["A new plant burning municipal waste","A new facility burning hazardous waste","A small-scale community siga for sanitation","A new unit burning biomedical waste"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ban covers burning municipal, biomedical and hazardous waste that emits poisonous and toxic fumes, but it does not apply to the traditional small-scale community method called siga. Crematoria and traditional agricultural, cultural, health and food preparation uses are also excepted. New waste plants have no such express exemption and are banned if the process emits those fumes, as held in MMDA v. Jancom.","source":"R.A. 8749, Sec. 20; MMDA v. Jancom Environmental Corp., G.R. No. 147465, January 30, 2002"},{"id":"gi-environment-06","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Under the Philippine Clean Water Act of 2004, which authorization specifies the quantity and quality of effluent a facility may release into a particular water body?","options":["The discharge permit","The wastewater charge","The environmental compliance certificate","The management area action plan"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The discharge permit is the legal authorization from the Department to discharge wastewater, and it specifies the allowed quantity and quality of effluent, the compliance schedule and monitoring requirements. The wastewater charge is a fee for discharging, not an authorization, and the environmental compliance certificate comes from P.D. 1586.","source":"R.A. 9275, Secs. 13, 14 and 15"},{"id":"gi-environment-07","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A company pollutes a river beyond the applicable standards, leaving it unfit for beneficial use. Under R.A. 9275, who is responsible for the clean-up?","options":["The local government, using its share of the national taxes","The national government, through a special clean-up fund","The downstream users who benefit from the restored river","The company that caused the pollution, at its own expense"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Any person who pollutes water bodies beyond the applicable standards must contain, remove and clean up the pollution at his own expense. The Department may conduct emergency clean-up if the polluter fails to act, but the polluter must reimburse the expenses.","source":"R.A. 9275, Sec. 16"},{"id":"gi-environment-08","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"R.A. 9003 requires the establishment of a Materials Recovery Facility in which of these?","options":["Every province or group of provinces","Every barangay or cluster of barangays","Every region or group of regions","Every legislative district in the country"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Materials Recovery Facility shall be established in every barangay or cluster of barangays, on barangay-owned or leased land or any suitable open space determined through the Sanggunian.","source":"R.A. 9003, Sec. 32"},{"id":"gi-environment-09","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What minimum waste diversion must each local government unit plan show within five years after the effectivity of R.A. 9003?","options":["At least 10 percent of all solid waste","At least 50 percent of all solid waste","At least 25 percent of all solid waste","At least 75 percent of all solid waste"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each plan must show diversion of at least 25 percent of all solid waste from disposal facilities within five years, through re-use, recycling, composting and other resource recovery, with goals increased every three years after that.","source":"R.A. 9003, Sec. 20"},{"id":"gi-environment-10","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Under the NIPAS law as amended, what term refers to identified areas outside and immediately adjacent to a protected area that need special development control?","options":["Natural monuments","Strict nature reserves","Resource reserves","Buffer zones"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Buffer zones are identified areas outside the boundaries of and immediately adjacent to designated protected areas that need special development control to avoid or minimize harm to the protected area.","source":"R.A. 7586, Sec. 4, as amended by R.A. 11038, Sec. 3"},{"id":"gi-environment-11","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Under the Philippine Clean Air Act of 1999, which of these is a stated basis for designating airsheds?","options":["Areas with similar climate, meteorology and topology","Areas belonging to a single legislative district","Areas with roughly equal population and income","Areas sharing one regional administrative office"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Airsheds are designated on the basis of areas with similar climate, meteorology and topology that affect how pollutants interchange and diffuse, or areas sharing common interests or similar development problems. Political or administrative boundaries are not the stated basis.","source":"R.A. 8749, Sec. 9"},{"id":"gi-environment-12","subject":"general","skill":"environment","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Under the Expanded NIPAS Act of 2018, who chairs the Protected Area Management Board?","options":["The governor of the province concerned","The DENR Regional Director with jurisdiction over the area","The Protected Area Superintendent of the area","The mayor of the municipality covering the area"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The DENR Regional Director under whose jurisdiction the protected area is located is the Chairperson. Governors are members, while the Protected Area Superintendent heads the management office that handles day-to-day administration.","source":"R.A. 7586, Secs. 11 and 11-B, as amended by R.A. 11038, Secs. 9 and 10"},{"id":"va-word-meaning-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"“The new drainage plan aims to mitigate the effects of flooding in low-lying barangays.” The word “mitigate” most nearly means:","options":["predict","explain","lessen","record"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To mitigate is to make something less severe, so “lessen” fits a plan meant to reduce flood damage. Predicting or recording floods would not reduce their effects."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which word is most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to “frugal”?","options":["extravagant","thrifty","prudent","modest"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A frugal person spends little, while an extravagant person spends too much. “Thrifty” is the trap: it is a synonym of frugal, not its opposite."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"“The inspector gave the documents only a perfunctory glance before signing the clearance.” The word “perfunctory” most nearly means:","options":["openly hostile","deeply suspicious","long and careful","quick and superficial"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Perfunctory describes something done as a routine, with little attention, so “quick and superficial” is right. The words “only” and “glance” also rule out “long and careful.”"},{"id":"va-word-meaning-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"“The delay in relief goods only exacerbated the tension in the evacuation center.” The word “exacerbated” most nearly means:","options":["settled","worsened","revealed","postponed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To exacerbate is to make a bad situation worse, so “worsened” is correct. “Revealed” is tempting, but the delay increased the tension instead of merely exposing it."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"“The new ordinance abrogates the earlier rule on street vending.” The word “abrogates” most nearly means:","options":["interprets","reinforces","repeals","publishes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To abrogate a law or rule is to abolish it formally, which is what “repeals” means. “Reinforces” says nearly the opposite."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"question":"“Dalisay ang hangarin ng guro na matulungan ang kaniyang mga mag-aaral.” Ano ang kasingkahulugan ng salitang “dalisay”?","options":["mapusok","wagas","maingat","tahimik"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ang dalisay ay nangangahulugang puro o walang halong ibang layunin, kaya “wagas” ang tamang sagot. Inilalarawan nito ang hangarin ng guro, hindi ang kaniyang pag-iingat o katahimikan."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"“Masalimuot ang proseso ng pagkuha ng permiso kaya maraming aplikante ang humihingi ng tulong.” Ano ang kahulugan ng salitang “masalimuot”?","options":["mabilis at madali","mahal at magastos","bago at moderno","magulo at mahirap unawain"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ang masalimuot ay nangangahulugang magulo o maraming sangkot na bahagi, kaya “magulo at mahirap unawain” ang tama. Lohikal na bunga nito ang paghingi ng tulong ng mga aplikante."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"“Nagpupuyos ang kalooban ng mga magsasaka nang malaman nilang inilipat sa iba ang kanilang lupa.” Ano ang ibig sabihin ng “nagpupuyos ang kalooban”?","options":["labis na nagagalit","lubos na natutuwa","labis na natatakot","lubhang nalulungkot"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ang nagpupuyos ay naglalarawan ng matinding galit na parang nag-aapoy sa loob, kaya “labis na nagagalit” ang tama. Maaaring malungkot din ang mga magsasaka, ngunit hindi lungkot ang isinasaad ng salita."},{"id":"va-word-meaning-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"word-meaning","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"question":"“Ayon sa hinuha ng imbestigador, nagmula ang sunog sa lumang kable sa kusina.” Ano ang kahulugan ng salitang “hinuha”?","options":["utos mula sa nakatataas","tala ng mga pangyayari","palagay batay sa katibayan","pangakong binitiwan sa publiko"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ang hinuha ay palagay o konklusyong nabuo mula sa mga nakitang katibayan, kaya “palagay batay sa katibayan” ang tama. Hindi ito simpleng tala, sapagkat may pagpapasiya ang imbestigador tungkol sa pinagmulan ng sunog."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"The applicant, together with her two sisters, ____ waiting outside the examination room.","options":["are","is","were","have been"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The subject is the singular “applicant”; a phrase introduced by “together with” does not change the number of the subject, so the verb is “is.”"},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Because the instructions were vague, many applicants were ____ about which form to submit.","options":["certain","delighted","indifferent","confused"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vague instructions naturally leave people unsure, so “confused” completes the cause and effect. “Certain” says the reverse of what the first clause leads you to expect."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"The typhoon destroyed the only bridge to the island; ____, relief goods had to be delivered by boat.","options":["consequently","nevertheless","likewise","for instance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Delivery by boat is the result of the bridge being destroyed, so a result word is needed: “consequently.” “Nevertheless” would signal a contrast that the two clauses do not have."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"If I ____ the head of the agency, I would simplify the application process.","options":["am","will be","were","have been"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The clause states an unreal present condition, shown by “would simplify” in the main clause, and that calls for the subjunctive “were.” The other forms do not pair with “would” in this kind of conditional."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"The senator's argument was so ____ that even her critics admitted it was hard to refute, for every claim rested on evidence and every objection was answered.","options":["verbose","cogent","evasive","inflammatory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cogent means clear, logical and convincing, which matches an argument its critics could not easily refute. “Verbose” only means wordy and says nothing about how strong the reasoning is."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"question":"Nag-aral siya ____ mabuti kaya pumasa siya sa pagsusulit.","options":["ng","sa","na","nang"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Ginagamit ang “nang” bago ang pang-abay na pamaraan, na nagsasaad kung paano ginawa ang kilos, kaya “nag-aral nang mabuti” ang wasto. Ang “ng” ay pananda ng pangngalan at hindi ng ganitong paglalarawan."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Hindi natuloy ang pagpupulong ____ malakas ang ulan sa buong lalawigan.","options":["sapagkat","upang","subalit","kung"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sanhi ng hindi pagkatuloy ng pagpupulong ang malakas na ulan, kaya pangatnig na nagsasaad ng dahilan ang kailangan: “sapagkat.” Ang “upang” ay para sa layunin at ang “subalit” ay para sa pagsalungat."},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Bukas pa ____ ng komisyon ang resulta ng pagsusulit.","options":["inilabas","nailabas","ilalabas","inilalabas"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ang “bukas” ay tumutukoy sa panahong darating, kaya aspektong kontemplatibo ang kailangan: “ilalabas.” Tapos na ang kilos sa “inilabas” at kasalukuyang nagaganap naman sa “inilalabas.”"},{"id":"va-sentence-completion-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-completion","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"question":"____ mo ang mga tuyong dahon bago mo diligan ang mga halaman.","options":["Walisan","Walisin","Magwalis","Nagwalis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Walisin” ang gamit kapag ang tinutukoy ay ang inaalis, gaya ng mga tuyong dahon, samantalang “walisan” kapag ang lugar ang nililinis, gaya ng bakuran. Hindi tumatanggap ng “mo” ang “magwalis” at “nagwalis” sa ganitong pangungusap."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which segment of the sentence contains an error? “The list of qualified applicants were posted on the bulletin board of the regional office last Monday.”","options":["The list of qualified applicants","were posted","on the bulletin board of the regional office","last Monday."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The subject is the singular “list,” not “applicants,” so the verb should be “was posted.” The noun nearest the verb is the trap."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which segment of the sentence contains an error? “Between you and I, the new schedule will not take effect until the director signs the memorandum.”","options":["Between you and I,","the new schedule will not","take effect until","the director signs the memorandum."],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Between” is a preposition and takes the object form, so the phrase should read “between you and me.” The present tense “signs” is correct after “until” when the main clause refers to the future."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which segment of the sentence contains an error? “Neither the auditors nor the supervisor have reviewed the liquidation report that was submitted yesterday.”","options":["Neither the auditors","that was submitted yesterday.","the liquidation report","nor the supervisor have reviewed"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With “neither...nor,” the verb agrees with the nearer subject. “Supervisor” is singular, so the correct form is “nor the supervisor has reviewed.”"},{"id":"va-error-recognition-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which segment of the sentence contains an error? “The records officer is responsible in maintaining all personnel files, which must be kept in a locked cabinet.”","options":["The records officer","is responsible in maintaining","all personnel files, which must","be kept in a locked cabinet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The idiom is “responsible for,” so the segment should read “is responsible for maintaining.” The other segments use standard prepositions and verb forms."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Which segment of the sentence contains an error? “Had the committee submitted its findings earlier, the agency would avoid the penalty imposed by the commission.”","options":["Had the committee submitted","its findings earlier,","the agency would avoid","the penalty imposed by the commission."],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Had the committee submitted” sets up an unreal past condition, so the main clause needs “would have avoided.” The inverted opening without “if” looks odd but is correct."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"question":"Aling bahagi ng pangungusap ang may mali? “Mataas raw ang presyo ng bigas sa pamilihan ngayong buwan, kaya nagtitipid ang mga mamimili.”","options":["Mataas raw ang presyo","ng bigas sa pamilihan","ngayong buwan,","kaya nagtitipid ang mga mamimili."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nagtatapos sa katinig ang “mataas,” kaya “daw” ang dapat na kasunod nito: “Mataas daw ang presyo.” Ginagamit ang “raw” pagkatapos ng patinig at ng malapatinig na w at y."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Aling bahagi ng pangungusap ang may mali? “Ang mga guro ay dumalo sa pulong at ang mga magulang ay dumalo din sa nasabing pagtitipon.”","options":["Ang mga guro ay dumalo","sa pulong at ang mga magulang","ay dumalo din","sa nasabing pagtitipon."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nagtatapos sa patinig ang “dumalo,” kaya “rin” ang wastong kasunod nito: “ay dumalo rin.” Ang “din” ay para sa mga salitang nagtatapos sa katinig maliban sa w at y."},{"id":"va-error-recognition-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Aling bahagi ng pangungusap ang may mali? “May ba kayong natanggap na abiso mula sa tanggapan ng alkalde kahapon?”","options":["na abiso mula sa","tanggapan ng alkalde","kahapon?","May ba kayong natanggap"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Hindi sinusundan ng katagang “ba” ang “may”; “mayroon” ang ginagamit kapag kataga o panghalip ang kasunod, kaya “Mayroon ba kayong natanggap” ang wasto. Direktang pangngalan, pandiwa o pang-uri ang sumusunod sa “may.”"},{"id":"va-error-recognition-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"error-recognition","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"question":"Aling bahagi ng pangungusap ang may mali? “Binigyan ng parangal nang alkalde ang mga guro nang matapos ang palatuntunan.”","options":["Binigyan ng parangal","nang alkalde ang mga guro","nang matapos","ang palatuntunan."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ang alkalde ang gumawa ng kilos, kaya “ng” ang pananda: “ng alkalde.” Tama ang “nang matapos” dahil nangangahulugan itong “noong” matapos, at ito ang bitag sa tanong."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which of the following is a complete sentence?","options":["Because the budget was approved late.","The budget that the council approved last week.","The council approved the budget last week.","Approving the budget during last week's session."],"answer":2,"explanation":"“The council approved the budget last week” has a subject and a finite verb and expresses a complete thought. The others are fragments: a dependent clause, a noun with a modifier, and a participial phrase."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Which sentence correctly joins the two independent clauses?","options":["The hearing ended late, the witnesses went home.","The hearing ended late the witnesses went home.","The hearing ended late so, the witnesses went home.","The hearing ended late, so the witnesses went home."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A comma plus the coordinating conjunction “so” correctly joins two independent clauses. The others are a comma splice, a run-on and a misplaced comma."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which sentence is free of a dangling modifier?","options":["Walking to the office, the clerk was drenched by the rain.","Walking to the office, the rain drenched the clerk.","Walking to the office, the clerk's clothes got drenched by the rain.","Drenched by the rain, the walk to the office seemed endless to the clerk."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An opening modifier must describe the subject that follows it, and the clerk is the one who can walk to the office. In the other sentences the modifier lands on the rain or the clothes, which cannot walk, or on the walk itself, which cannot be drenched."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which sentence shows correct parallel structure?","options":["The officer must draft reports, attending hearings, and answer queries.","The officer must draft reports, attend hearings, and answer queries.","The officer must draft reports, attend hearings, and answering queries.","The officer must drafting reports, attend hearings, and answer queries."],"answer":1,"explanation":"After “must,” all three verbs should be in the same base form: draft, attend and answer. Each of the other versions slips in a single -ing form and so breaks the series."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Which sentence uses the opening “Not only” correctly?","options":["Not only the agency reduced its fees, but it also shortened its processing time.","Not only did the agency reduced its fees, but it also shortened its processing time.","Not only did the agency reduce its fees, but also shortening its processing time.","Not only did the agency reduce its fees, but it also shortened its processing time."],"answer":3,"explanation":"An opening “Not only” requires inversion with a helping verb and a base form, “did the agency reduce,” and the second clause must be complete and parallel. The other versions miss the inversion, double the past tense, or switch to an -ing form."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"question":"Alin sa mga pangungusap ang nasa di-karaniwang ayos?","options":["Ang mga mag-aaral ay naglinis ng silid-aklatan.","Naglinis ng silid-aklatan ang mga mag-aaral.","Nagsumite ng ulat ang kawani kahapon.","Bumili ng bagong kagamitan ang paaralan."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nasa di-karaniwang ayos ang pangungusap kapag nauuna ang paksa sa panaguri at pinag-uugnay ang dalawa ng “ay.” Nauuna naman ang panaguri sa tatlong iba pang pangungusap, kaya karaniwan ang ayos ng mga ito."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Alin ang wastong pagkakabuo ng pangungusap?","options":["Si Ana at Ben ay dumalo sa seminar kahapon.","Sina Ana at Ben ay dadalo sa seminar kahapon.","Sina Ana at Ben ay dumalo sa seminar kahapon.","Sina Ana at si Ben ay dumadalo sa seminar kahapon."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Iisang pananda lamang ang ginamit para sa dalawang tao, kaya maramihang “sina” ang kailangan, at aspektong naganap na “dumalo” ang tugma sa “kahapon.” Mali ang “Si Ana at Ben” dahil isahan ang pananda, at hindi tugma sa “kahapon” ang “dadalo” at “dumadalo.”"},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"question":"Alin ang pangungusap na wasto ang gamit ng pananda at ng pandiwa?","options":["Ayon sa Gng. Reyes, ilalabas bukas ang listahan ng mga nakapasa.","Ayon kay Gng. Reyes, ilalabas bukas ang listahan ng mga nakapasa.","Ayon ni Gng. Reyes, ilalabas bukas ang listahan ng mga nakapasa.","Ayon kay Gng. Reyes, ilalabas kahapon ang listahan ng mga nakapasa."],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Ayon kay” ang gamit bago ang pangalan ng isang tao, at “ayon sa” naman bago ang karaniwang pangngalan. Hindi rin tugma ang pandiwang “ilalabas” sa “kahapon,” kaya “bukas” ang wasto."},{"id":"va-sentence-structure-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"sentence-structure","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"question":"Alin ang pangungusap na wasto ang pokus ng pandiwa?","options":["Ang liham ay sumulat ng kalihim para sa alkalde.","Ang kalihim ay isinulat ang liham para sa alkalde.","Ang liham ay nagsulat ang kalihim para sa alkalde.","Ang liham ay isinulat ng kalihim para sa alkalde."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Liham ang paksa, kaya pandiwang nakapokus sa layon ang kailangan: “isinulat,” at “ng kalihim” ang tagaganap. Pokus sa tagaganap ang “sumulat” at “nagsulat,” kaya hindi ito tugma sa paksang liham."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"S. First, the applicant fills out the request form at the barangay hall.\nT. Applying for a barangay business clearance takes only a few steps.\nU. Finally, the clearance is released once the barangay captain signs it.\nV. Next, the applicant pays the fee at the treasurer's window.","question":"Arrange the sentences to form a coherent paragraph.","options":["T-S-V-U","S-T-V-U","T-V-S-U","S-V-T-U"],"answer":0,"explanation":"T introduces the topic, and the signal words then give the sequence: First (S), Next (V), Finally (U)."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"S. The same roots also trap sediment, which keeps shorelines from eroding.\nT. For these reasons, several coastal towns now treat mangrove planting as a form of disaster preparedness.\nU. Mangrove forests protect coastal communities in more ways than one.\nV. Their dense roots slow down waves and reduce the damage caused by storm surges.","question":"Arrange the sentences into the most logical paragraph.","options":["U-S-V-T","V-U-S-T","U-V-S-T","U-V-T-S"],"answer":2,"explanation":"U states the main idea, V gives the first benefit, and S adds a second one with “the same roots also,” which must follow V. T concludes with “for these reasons.”"},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"S. When the town's only public library flooded three years ago, most of its books were lost.\nT. Their collection soon outgrew the borrowed classroom that housed it.\nU. Last month, the municipality finally opened a larger library building on higher ground.\nV. Volunteers responded by collecting donated books from nearby schools and families.","question":"Choose the order that makes the passage flow in time.","options":["V-S-T-U","S-V-T-U","S-T-V-U","S-V-U-T"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The story runs in time: the flood (S), the volunteers' response (V), the growth of “their collection” (T), then the new building last month (U). “Their” in T needs the volunteers in V as its reference."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"S. With such a system, a citizen would need to present a document only once.\nT. This repetition costs applicants both time and transport money.\nU. One remedy is a shared database that lets offices verify records among themselves.\nV. Many government offices still require citizens to submit the same documents again and again.","question":"Which order presents the problem and its solution most logically?","options":["T-V-U-S","V-U-T-S","V-T-S-U","V-T-U-S"],"answer":3,"explanation":"V states the problem, T explains its cost through “this repetition,” U offers the remedy, and S describes the effect of “such a system.” Each pointing word depends on the sentence before it."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"S. Supporters argue that the shift lowers both fuel costs and street-level pollution.\nT. A fair evaluation must therefore weigh long-term expenses against immediate benefits.\nU. Several cities have begun replacing gasoline tricycles with electric ones.\nV. Critics, however, note that these savings shrink once the cost of replacing batteries is counted.","question":"Determine the sequence that best develops the argument.","options":["U-S-V-T","S-U-V-T","U-V-S-T","U-S-T-V"],"answer":0,"explanation":"U supplies the “shift” that S refers to, V answers S with “however” and “these savings,” and T draws the conclusion from both sides with “therefore.” Placing V before S would leave “these savings” with nothing to refer to."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"passage":"S. Pagkatapos, itanim ang mga punla nang may sapat na pagitan.\nT. Una, ihanda ang lupa at tiyaking maluwag at mataba ito.\nU. Madali lamang magtanim ng kamatis sa bakuran.\nV. Panghuli, diligan ang mga ito araw-araw hanggang sa mamunga.","question":"Ayusin ang mga pangungusap upang makabuo ng maayos na talata.","options":["T-U-S-V","U-S-T-V","T-S-V-U","U-T-S-V"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Inilalahad ng U ang paksa, at sinusundan ito ng mga hakbang na may hudyat: Una (T), Pagkatapos (S) at Panghuli (V)."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"passage":"S. Sa pamamagitan nito, direkta na nilang naibebenta ang kanilang huli sa pamilihan.\nT. Ang dagdag na kitang ito ang ginagamit nila ngayon sa pagpapaaral ng kanilang mga anak.\nU. Dahil wala nang namamagitang ahente, tumaas ang kanilang kita.\nV. Nagkaisa ang mga mangingisda ng isang bayan sa baybayin sa pagbuo ng kooperatiba.","question":"Alin ang pinakamaayos na pagkakasunod-sunod ng mga pangungusap?","options":["S-V-U-T","V-U-S-T","V-S-U-T","V-S-T-U"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Binabanggit muna ng V ang kooperatiba, na tinutukoy ng “nito” sa S. Bunga ng direktang pagbebenta ang pagtaas ng kita sa U, at ang “dagdag na kitang ito” sa T ang huling ugnay."},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"passage":"S. Bukod dito, nakapipinsala rin ito sa mga hayop sa dagat na napagkakamalan itong pagkain.\nT. Malaking suliranin sa maraming lungsod ang basurang plastik.\nU. Bumabara ito sa mga kanal kaya mabilis bumaha kapag malakas ang ulan.\nV. Kaya naman, hinihikayat ng pamahalaan ang mga mamamayan na magdala ng sariling bayong sa pamilihan.","question":"Piliin ang ayos ng mga pangungusap na bumubuo ng lohikal na talata.","options":["T-S-U-V","T-U-S-V","U-T-S-V","T-U-V-S"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inilalahad ng T ang suliranin at ibinibigay ng U ang unang pinsala. Idinaragdag ng S ang ikalawang pinsala sa pamamagitan ng “Bukod dito,” at nagwawakas ang V sa tugon ng pamahalaan na may hudyat na “Kaya naman.”"},{"id":"va-paragraph-organization-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"paragraph-organization","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"passage":"S. Marami ang naniniwalang sapat na ang sipag upang umasenso sa buhay.\nT. Samakatuwid, kailangang samahan ang sipag ng mga patakarang nagbubukas ng pagkakataon para sa lahat.\nU. Halimbawa, ang masipag na magsasakang walang sariling lupa at puhunan ay nananatiling baon sa utang.\nV. Totoong mahalaga ang sipag, ngunit hindi ito laging nagbubunga kung walang pagkakataon.","question":"Tukuyin ang pagkakasunod-sunod na pinakamahusay na bumubuo sa pangangatwiran.","options":["S-V-U-T","V-S-U-T","S-U-V-T","S-V-T-U"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inilalahad ng S ang karaniwang paniniwala at sinasalungat ito ng V. Nagbibigay ang U ng halimbawa ng kawalan ng pagkakataon na binanggit sa V, at nagwawakas ang T sa konklusyong may hudyat na “Samakatuwid.”"},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-01","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"When one upland town began rationing piped water during the dry season, its local government did not immediately look for a new water source. Instead, it offered small grants to households that installed rainwater tanks, and it required all new public buildings to include one. Within four years, demand on the town's reservoir during the dry months fell by nearly a fifth. Officials concede that tanks alone cannot supply drinking water, since collected rain must still be treated before it is safe to consume. Even so, using stored rain for washing and gardening has left more of the reservoir for the uses that need it most.","question":"How did the local government respond to water rationing?","options":["It looked for a new source of piped water.","It built a second reservoir for the dry months.","It treated collected rain to make it drinkable.","It promoted and required rainwater tanks."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The passage says the town gave grants for household tanks and required them in new public buildings. It explicitly says the town did not immediately look for a new source."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-02","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"When one upland town began rationing piped water during the dry season, its local government did not immediately look for a new water source. Instead, it offered small grants to households that installed rainwater tanks, and it required all new public buildings to include one. Within four years, demand on the town's reservoir during the dry months fell by nearly a fifth. Officials concede that tanks alone cannot supply drinking water, since collected rain must still be treated before it is safe to consume. Even so, using stored rain for washing and gardening has left more of the reservoir for the uses that need it most.","question":"Why does stored rain help the town even though it is unsafe to drink untreated?","options":["It leaves more reservoir water for essential uses.","It lowers the cost of treating reservoir water.","It removes the need for further household grants.","It raises the reservoir above its normal level."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The last sentence says that using rain for washing and gardening leaves more reservoir water for the uses that need it most. Treatment costs and reservoir levels above normal are never mentioned."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-03","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"When one upland town began rationing piped water during the dry season, its local government did not immediately look for a new water source. Instead, it offered small grants to households that installed rainwater tanks, and it required all new public buildings to include one. Within four years, demand on the town's reservoir during the dry months fell by nearly a fifth. Officials concede that tanks alone cannot supply drinking water, since collected rain must still be treated before it is safe to consume. Even so, using stored rain for washing and gardening has left more of the reservoir for the uses that need it most.","question":"With which statement would the author most likely agree?","options":["Rainwater tanks should eventually replace reservoirs in most towns.","Government grants are wasted unless paired with strict penalties.","A shortage can sometimes be eased by reducing demand on existing supply.","Water rationing is the most effective response to any dry season."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The passage shows the town easing the strain by cutting demand on its existing reservoir instead of first looking for a new source. Replacing reservoirs goes too far, since the author notes that tanks alone cannot supply drinking water."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-04","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"Many people assume that a long report shows more effort than a short one. Experienced managers often think otherwise. A short report forces its writer to decide what matters, to discard what does not, and to state conclusions plainly. A long report, by contrast, can hide uncertain thinking behind tables and qualifications, leaving the reader to do the sorting the writer avoided. This is not an argument against detail, since evidence belongs in an annex where it can be checked. It is an argument for placing the decision the reader must make on the first page.","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Long reports are usually inaccurate and should not be trusted.","A good report states what matters first and keeps details checkable.","Managers dislike evidence and prefer to decide without reading it.","Reports should leave out tables and supporting details altogether."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The passage favors putting the decision up front while keeping evidence in an annex. Leaving out details is the trap, because the author says this is not an argument against detail."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-05","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"Many people assume that a long report shows more effort than a short one. Experienced managers often think otherwise. A short report forces its writer to decide what matters, to discard what does not, and to state conclusions plainly. A long report, by contrast, can hide uncertain thinking behind tables and qualifications, leaving the reader to do the sorting the writer avoided. This is not an argument against detail, since evidence belongs in an annex where it can be checked. It is an argument for placing the decision the reader must make on the first page.","question":"As used in the passage, the word “qualifications” most nearly means:","options":["educational credentials of the writer","official requirements for a post","tests of an employee's ability","statements that limit or hedge a claim"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A long report hides “uncertain thinking” behind them, so qualifications here are hedging statements that limit a claim. The everyday sense of credentials is the trap, since the sentence is about what fills a report and not about the writer's background."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-06","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"fil","difficulty":1,"passage":"Noong nakaraang taon, nagsimulang magtanim ng kawayan ang mga magsasaka sa isang bayan sa gilid ng ilog. Madalas kasing gumuho ang pampang tuwing tag-ulan, kaya natatangay ng tubig ang bahagi ng kanilang bukirin. Kumakapit sa lupa ang mga ugat ng kawayan, kaya unti-unting tumitibay ang pampang. Bukod dito, naibebenta rin ang magugulang na tangkay sa mga gumagawa ng muwebles. Inaamin ng mga magsasaka na matagal bago makita ang buong pakinabang ng kawayan, ngunit naniniwala silang sulit ang paghihintay dahil napoprotektahan na ang kanilang lupa, at nagkakaroon pa sila ng dagdag na kita.","question":"Bakit nagtanim ng kawayan ang mga magsasaka?","options":["Upang makapagbukas ng pagawaan ng muwebles","Upang mapalitan ang palay na hindi na kumikita","Upang mapatibay ang pampang na madalas gumuho","Upang mapigil ang pagtaas ng tubig sa ilog"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Isinasaad ng talata na gumuguho ang pampang kaya natatangay ang bukirin, at pinatitibay ito ng mga ugat ng kawayan. Karagdagang pakinabang lamang ang pagbebenta sa mga gumagawa ng muwebles."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-07","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"passage":"Noong nakaraang taon, nagsimulang magtanim ng kawayan ang mga magsasaka sa isang bayan sa gilid ng ilog. Madalas kasing gumuho ang pampang tuwing tag-ulan, kaya natatangay ng tubig ang bahagi ng kanilang bukirin. Kumakapit sa lupa ang mga ugat ng kawayan, kaya unti-unting tumitibay ang pampang. Bukod dito, naibebenta rin ang magugulang na tangkay sa mga gumagawa ng muwebles. Inaamin ng mga magsasaka na matagal bago makita ang buong pakinabang ng kawayan, ngunit naniniwala silang sulit ang paghihintay dahil napoprotektahan na ang kanilang lupa, at nagkakaroon pa sila ng dagdag na kita.","question":"Ano ang maihihinuha tungkol sa pananaw ng mga magsasaka?","options":["Handa silang maghintay para sa pangmatagalang pakinabang.","Nagsisisi silang hindi agad kumita sa kawayan.","Umaasa silang iiwan na ang pagsasaka balang araw.","Mas mahalaga sa kanila ang kita kaysa sa kanilang lupa."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inaamin nilang matagal bago makita ang buong pakinabang ngunit sinasabi nilang sulit ang paghihintay, kaya handa silang maghintay. Walang bahagi ng talata ang nagpapakita ng pagsisisi o ng balak na iwan ang pagsasaka."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-08","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"fil","difficulty":2,"passage":"Madalas sabihing tanda ng kahinaan ng isang wika ang panghihiram ng salita. Kabaligtaran nito ang ipinakikita ng karanasan. Humihiram ang isang buhay na wika dahil may mga bagong bagay at kaisipang kailangan nitong pangalanan. Kapag tinanggap na ng mga nagsasalita ang hiram na salita, inaangkop nila ito sa sariling bigkas at baybay, hanggang sa hindi na ito maramdamang dayuhan. Ang wikang tumatangging humiram ay nanganganib na mawalan ng salita para sa mga bagong gawain ng lipunan, kaya unti-unti itong napapalitan ng ibang wika sa mga larangang iyon.","question":"Ano ang pangunahing kaisipan ng talata?","options":["Dapat iwasan ang panghihiram upang manatiling dalisay ang wika.","Mahirap baybayin sa sariling wika ang mga salitang hiram.","Nawawala ang mga wikang madalas manghiram ng salita.","Tanda ng sigla ng wika ang panghihiram at pag-angkop ng salita."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sinasalungat ng talata ang paniniwalang kahinaan ang panghihiram at ipinakikita nitong kailangan ito ng buhay na wika. Kabaligtaran nito ang pagsasabing nawawala ang mga wikang madalas manghiram, sapagkat ang tumatangging humiram ang itinuturing na nanganganib."},{"id":"va-reading-comprehension-09","subject":"verbal","skill":"reading-comprehension","lang":"fil","difficulty":3,"passage":"Madalas sabihing tanda ng kahinaan ng isang wika ang panghihiram ng salita. Kabaligtaran nito ang ipinakikita ng karanasan. Humihiram ang isang buhay na wika dahil may mga bagong bagay at kaisipang kailangan nitong pangalanan. Kapag tinanggap na ng mga nagsasalita ang hiram na salita, inaangkop nila ito sa sariling bigkas at baybay, hanggang sa hindi na ito maramdamang dayuhan. Ang wikang tumatangging humiram ay nanganganib na mawalan ng salita para sa mga bagong gawain ng lipunan, kaya unti-unti itong napapalitan ng ibang wika sa mga larangang iyon.","question":"Alin ang pinakaangkop na halimbawa ng pag-angkop na binabanggit sa talata?","options":["Paggamit ng salitang Ingles nang walang pagbabago sa baybay","Pagbaybay ng salitang Ingles na computer bilang kompyuter","Pag-imbento ng ganap na bagong salita para sa isang makina","Pagsasalin ng isang buong aklat mula sa Ingles tungo sa Filipino"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inilalarawan ng talata ang pag-angkop bilang pagbabago ng hiram na salita ayon sa sariling bigkas at baybay, na makikita sa “kompyuter.” Hindi ito panghihiram ng salita kung bago ang iniimbento o kung buong aklat ang isinasalin."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-01","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Cartographer is to map as choreographer is to ____.","options":["stage","music","dance","costume"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cartographer creates maps and a choreographer creates dances. A choreographer may work with music or on a stage, but neither is what the choreographer makes."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-02","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Thermometer is to temperature as odometer is to ____.","options":["distance","speed","fuel","pressure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each instrument is paired with what it measures, and an odometer measures distance travelled. Speed is the trap because it is read by the speedometer, a different dial."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-03","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Petal is to flower as spoke is to ____.","options":["road","axle","tire","wheel"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The relationship is part to whole: a petal is part of a flower and a spoke is part of a wheel. An axle and a tire are also wheel-related, but a spoke is not a part of either."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-04","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Drought is to water as famine is to ____.","options":["hunger","food","crop","death"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A drought is a severe lack of water and a famine is a severe lack of food. Hunger is the trap because it is a result of famine, not the thing that is lacking."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-05","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Frugal is to extravagant as candid is to ____.","options":["honest","evasive","timid","cheerful"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pairs are opposites: frugal is the opposite of extravagant, and candid, meaning open and direct, is the opposite of evasive. Honest is a near synonym of candid, so it reverses the relationship."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-06","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Scalpel is to surgeon as trowel is to ____.","options":["mason","tailor","painter","plumber"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each tool is paired with the worker who uses it, and a mason uses a trowel to spread mortar. The other workers have their own characteristic tools, such as needles, brushes and wrenches."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-07","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Breeze is to gale as drizzle is to ____.","options":["mist","puddle","downpour","umbrella"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The second word is a much stronger form of the first: a gale is a very strong wind and a downpour is very heavy rain. Mist runs the wrong way because it is lighter than drizzle."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-08","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Statute is to legislator as blueprint is to ____.","options":["carpenter","surveyor","contractor","architect"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A legislator drafts a statute and an architect draws up a blueprint, so each pair links a document to its author. Carpenters and contractors carry out a blueprint rather than create it."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-09","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Ravenous is to hungry as exhausted is to ____.","options":["energetic","tired","sleeping","restless"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The first word is the extreme degree of the second: ravenous means extremely hungry and exhausted means extremely tired. Energetic is an opposite, not a milder form."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-10","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Insomnia is to sleep as amnesia is to ____.","options":["memory","illness","dream","confusion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Insomnia is the inability to sleep and amnesia is the loss of memory, so each pair links a condition to what is missing. Confusion may accompany amnesia, but it is not what the person has lost."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-11","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Sanctimonious is to piety as pedantic is to ____.","options":["humility","ignorance","learning","patience"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A sanctimonious person makes an annoying show of piety and a pedantic person makes an annoying show of learning. Ignorance is the trap because a pedant parades knowledge rather than lacks it."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-12","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Inexorable is to persuade as impregnable is to ____.","options":["defend","conceive","admire","capture"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Someone inexorable cannot be persuaded and a fortress that is impregnable cannot be captured. Conceive is a trap based only on the sound of the word, and defend fails because an impregnable place is easy to defend."},{"id":"an-word-analogy-13","subject":"analytical","skill":"word-analogy","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Vaccine is to infection as firewall is to ____.","options":["computer","intrusion","password","network"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A vaccine guards against infection and a firewall guards against intrusion, so each pair links a safeguard to the threat it blocks. A network is what the firewall protects, not what it blocks."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-01","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What letter comes next in the series? B, E, H, K, ____","options":["M","O","N","L"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each letter moves forward three places in the alphabet: B, E, H, K, and three places after K is N."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-02","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What symbol comes next in the series? ★ ★ ○ ★ ★ ○ ★ ____","options":["○","★","□","△"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The group of two stars and one circle repeats. The last group has only one star so far, so a second star comes next, then the circle."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-03","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"In a code, PLANT is written as QMBOU. Using the same rule, how is STONE written?","options":["TUPOF","RSNMD","TUONF","UVQPG"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each letter is replaced by the one right after it in the alphabet, so S, T, O, N, E become T, U, P, O, F. RSNMD is the trap of shifting backward instead of forward."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-04","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"All auditors are accountants. Rina is an auditor. Which conclusion must be true?","options":["All accountants are auditors.","Some accountants are not auditors.","Rina is a senior accountant.","Rina is an accountant."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Rina belongs to the group of auditors, and that whole group lies inside the group of accountants, so she is an accountant. All accountants are auditors reverses the premise, which does not follow."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-05","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"If the memo is approved, then the budget is released. The budget was not released. Which conclusion must be true?","options":["The memo was approved.","The memo is under review.","The memo was not approved.","The budget will come later."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the result of a conditional is false, its condition must also be false, a valid form called denying the consequent. Approval would have released the budget, so the memo was not approved."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-06","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"If it rains, the match is cancelled. The match was cancelled. What follows from these two statements?","options":["It is uncertain whether it rained.","It certainly rained that day.","It certainly did not rain.","Only rain can cancel the match."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The statement says rain is enough to cancel the match, not that it is the only possible cause, so the match may have been cancelled for another reason. Concluding that it rained is the fallacy of affirming the consequent."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-07","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Some clerks are typists. All typists are encoders. Which conclusion must be true?","options":["All clerks are encoders.","Some clerks are encoders.","All encoders are clerks.","No typist is a clerk."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The clerks who are typists are also encoders, because every typist is an encoder, so at least some clerks are encoders. Nothing is known about the clerks who are not typists, which rules out the claim about all clerks."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-08","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"In a code, @ means multiply, # means add and % means subtract. Working strictly from left to right, what is the value of 6 @ 3 # 4 % 2?","options":["16","22","24","20"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Read as 6 times 3, plus 4, minus 2: 6 times 3 is 18, adding 4 gives 22, and subtracting 2 gives 20. The value 22 is the trap of stopping before the last step."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-09","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"In a code, each letter is replaced by the letter holding the same position counted from the other end of the alphabet, so A becomes Z and B becomes Y. How is MAP written?","options":["NZK","NAK","OZK","NZL"],"answer":0,"explanation":"M is the 13th letter from the start, and the 13th from the end is N. A becomes Z, and P, the 16th letter, becomes K, the 16th from the end, which gives NZK."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-10","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What letter comes next in the series? A, Z, C, X, E, V, G, ____","options":["S","T","U","I"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two series alternate: A, C, E, G moves forward by two, while Z, X, V moves backward by two. The next term belongs to the backward series, and two places before V is T."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-11","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"No inspector is corrupt. Some officials are inspectors. Which conclusion must be true?","options":["Some officials are corrupt.","No official is corrupt.","Some officials are not corrupt.","All officials are inspectors."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The officials who are inspectors cannot be corrupt, so at least some officials are not corrupt. The statements say nothing about the other officials, so it cannot be concluded that no official is corrupt or that some officials are corrupt."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-12","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"The arrow means if-then and ~ means not. Given: P → Q, Q → R, ~R.","question":"If all three statements are true, which must also be true?","options":["P is true and Q is false.","P and Q are both false.","P is false and Q is true.","P and Q are both true."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since R is false, Q must be false, because a true Q would force R to be true. Since Q is false, P must be false for the same reason, so both are false."},{"id":"an-symbolic-logic-13","subject":"analytical","skill":"symbolic-logic","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Which of these arguments is logically valid?","options":["All nurses are licensed. Ana is licensed. So Ana is a nurse.","If a file is saved, its icon turns green. It was not saved. So its icon is not green.","Some nurses are tall. Some tall people sing. So some nurses sing.","Either the report is late or the chief is away. He is not away. So it is late."],"answer":3,"explanation":"When one of two alternatives is ruled out, the other must hold, so the argument about the late report is valid. The others have invalid forms: affirming the consequent with Ana, denying the antecedent with the file, and, with the nurses, an unsupported link between two statements that each cover only some people."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-01","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"The municipal library will open on Sundays starting next month. Residents who work on weekdays have often asked for weekend access.","question":"Which assumption underlies the decision to open on Sundays?","options":["The library is overcrowded on weekdays.","Residents prefer online books to print.","Some weekday workers would come on Sundays.","Library staff earn extra pay on Sundays."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The change only makes sense if at least some of the residents who asked would actually use the library on Sundays. The passage says nothing about overcrowding, online books or staff pay."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-02","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"Every applicant for the post must pass both a written test and an interview. Marco passed the interview but failed the written test.","question":"Which conclusion follows from the passage?","options":["Marco did not prepare for the written test.","Marco will retake the written test next year.","The interview is easier than the written test.","Marco has not met the requirements for the post."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both tests are required and Marco failed one, so he has not met the requirements. The passage gives no information about his preparation, his future plans or the relative difficulty of the tests."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-03","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"Barangay San Isidro posted flood warnings after three days of rain. Families living along the creek were told to move to the covered court by evening.","question":"Which conclusion is best supported by the passage?","options":["Officials saw the creek area as being at higher risk.","The creek has flooded every year for a decade.","The covered court was built to serve as a shelter.","All families in the barangay had to leave their homes."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Singling out families along the creek shows that officials saw that area as more at risk. The passage reports the instruction only for those families, so it cannot be said that every family in the barangay had to leave."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-04","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"passage":"All 40 employees of the records section attended the data privacy seminar on Friday. Liza is an employee of the records section.","question":"Which conclusion follows from the passage?","options":["Liza handles the most sensitive records.","Only records staff attended the seminar.","Liza attended the data privacy seminar.","The seminar was required by national law."],"answer":2,"explanation":"All records section employees attended and Liza is one of them, so she attended. Saying that only records staff attended goes too far, because the passage does not rule out other attendees."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-05","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"To cut long queues, the city hall will add two more cashiers to its payment counter during peak hours.","question":"Which assumption does this plan rely on?","options":["Queues are longest at the start of each month.","The new cashiers will be hired from outside.","Residents would rather pay online than in person.","Too few cashiers is one cause of the long queues."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adding cashiers can only shorten queues if too few cashiers is part of the problem. The other statements could all be false and the plan would still make sense."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-06","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"An advertisement states: Our water filter removes 99 percent of bacteria, so your family will never again get sick from drinking water.","question":"Which assumption does the advertisement make?","options":["Nothing the filter leaves behind can cause illness.","Families drink more water when it is filtered.","The filter costs less than buying bottled water.","Most bacteria in water are harmless to adults."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The promise of never getting sick holds only if the remaining bacteria and anything else the filter misses are harmless. The trap about most bacteria being harmless would make the filter less necessary, and the claim does not depend on it."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-07","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"In a survey of 200 commuters at a Cubao terminal, 150 said they ride the bus at least five days a week.","question":"Which conclusion can validly be drawn from the survey?","options":["Most Filipinos ride the bus at least five days a week.","Most respondents said they ride the bus five or more days a week.","Buses are cheaper than other forms of transport in the city.","Fifty of those surveyed never ride the bus on any day at all."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since 150 of 200 is 75 percent, most of those surveyed gave that answer. Extending the result to all Filipinos overgeneralizes from one terminal, and the other 50 may still ride the bus on fewer days."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-08","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"Since a bike lane opened on Rizal Avenue, the number of cyclists there has tripled. The council concludes that building similar lanes on other avenues will also increase cycling.","question":"Which assumption does the council's conclusion require?","options":["Cycling is safer than riding a jeepney on any avenue.","Drivers on Rizal Avenue support the new bike lane.","The lane on Rizal Avenue cost less than was budgeted.","Other avenues resemble Rizal Avenue in relevant ways."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The council is reasoning by analogy, so the result carries over only if the other avenues are similar in the ways that matter for cycling. Safety comparisons, driver support and cost are not needed for that inference."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-09","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"In the regional office, every employee who finished the records training received a certificate. Ana, an employee of the regional office, did not receive a certificate.","question":"Which conclusion must be true?","options":["Ana did not finish the records training.","Ana never enrolled in the records training.","Ana failed the records training exam.","The certificates were released late."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Finishing the training guarantees a certificate, so an employee with no certificate did not finish. She may have enrolled and stopped, so the claims that she never enrolled and that she failed an exam both go beyond the passage."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-10","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"passage":"Tourist arrivals in the town rose sharply after the new highway opened. The mayor argues that the highway caused the rise.","question":"Which assumption does the mayor's argument rely on?","options":["Tourists spend more now than they did before.","The highway was completed on schedule.","No other factor accounts for the rise in arrivals.","Residents benefited from the increase in tourism."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Treating something that came first as the cause assumes no other event, such as a festival or a promotion, explains the rise. Tourist spending, benefits to residents and the highway's completion date have no bearing on the cause."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-11","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"An agency has removed its paper forms and now accepts applications only through its website. Officials expect the number of applications received to stay the same.","question":"Which assumption does the officials' expectation depend on?","options":["Online processing is cheaper than manual processing.","Those who used paper forms can access the website.","Website applications contain fewer errors than paper.","Other agencies have already stopped using paper forms."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number of applications can hold steady only if former paper applicants are able to use the website. Cost and error rates are possible benefits of the change, but the expectation does not rest on them."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-12","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"Last year, the three barangays with the most tanods recorded the fewest thefts. The council therefore plans to hire more tanods in all barangays to reduce theft.","question":"Which assumption does the council's reasoning depend on?","options":["Those three barangays are wealthier than the others.","Theft is the most common crime in every barangay.","Tanods are more effective than police officers.","Having more tanods helped to lower theft there."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The plan treats a correlation as cause and effect, so it assumes the tanods actually contributed to the lower theft. If those barangays were simply wealthier, that would be a rival explanation that weakens the plan, not an assumption it needs."},{"id":"an-assumptions-conclusions-13","subject":"analytical","skill":"assumptions-conclusions","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"passage":"Of the 60 applicants for a post, 45 passed the written test. Only those who passed the written test could take the interview, and 30 people passed the interview.","question":"Which conclusion must be true?","options":["Exactly half of the applicants passed both stages.","Exactly 15 applicants took and failed the interview.","One third of those who took the interview failed it.","The interview is harder to pass than the written test."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Everyone who passed the interview had already passed the written test, so exactly 30 of the 60 passed both. The trap is assuming all 45 who passed the written test took the interview; some of the 15 among them who did not pass it may have skipped it, so the number who failed is unknown."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-01","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"table":{"caption":"Applications received by a regional office","headers":["Month","Applications"],"rows":[["January","120"],["February","150"],["March","135"],["April","180"],["May","165"]]},"question":"In which month did the office receive the most applications?","options":["February","May","April","March"],"answer":2,"explanation":"April has the largest figure in the table at 180 applications, ahead of May at 165."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-02","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"table":{"caption":"Office supplies purchased","headers":["Item","Quantity","Unit price (PHP)"],"rows":[["Bond paper (ream)","20","250"],["Ballpen (box)","15","120"],["Folder (pack)","10","90"],["Stapler","5","180"]]},"question":"How much was spent on ballpens?","options":["PHP 1,800","PHP 1,500","PHP 2,000","PHP 1,350"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiply quantity by unit price: 15 boxes times PHP 120 is PHP 1,800."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-03","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Annual budget by department (in thousand pesos)","headers":["Department","Budget"],"rows":[["Administration","450"],["Finance","300"],["Human Resources","150"],["Operations","600"]]},"question":"What percent of the total budget went to Operations?","options":["30%","40%","45%","60%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The total budget is 1,500 thousand pesos, and 600 divided by 1,500 is 40%. The value 60% is the trap of reading the budget figure itself as a percentage."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-04","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Trainees enrolled by course","headers":["Course","Men","Women"],"rows":[["Encoding","30","50"],["Bookkeeping","25","35"],["Welding","65","15"]]},"question":"Across all three courses, what is the ratio of women to men?","options":["6:5","3:4","2:3","5:6"],"answer":3,"explanation":"There are 100 women and 120 men in total, and 100:120 simplifies to 5:6. The ratio 6:5 reverses the order the question asks for."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-05","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Monthly rainfall in a town","headers":["Month","Rainfall (mm)"],"rows":[["June","240"],["July","310"],["August","280"],["September","250"],["October","170"]]},"question":"What is the average monthly rainfall for the five months?","options":["240 mm","260 mm","250 mm","280 mm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The five figures add up to 1,250 mm, and 1,250 divided by 5 is 250 mm."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-06","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Annual sales of a cooperative","headers":["Year","Sales (PHP)"],"rows":[["2022","80,000"],["2023","100,000"],["2024","120,000"],["2025","150,000"]]},"question":"What was the percent increase in sales from 2022 to 2023?","options":["20%","25%","30%","15%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sales rose by PHP 20,000 from a base of PHP 80,000, and 20,000 divided by 80,000 is 25%. The value 20% is the trap of dividing by the later year instead of the base year."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-07","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Results of a licensing examination by region","headers":["Region","Examinees","Passers"],"rows":[["Region A","400","100"],["Region B","500","75"],["Region C","250","50"],["Region D","200","60"]]},"question":"Which region had the highest passing rate?","options":["Region D","Region A","Region C","Region B"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The passing rates are 25% for Region A, 15% for Region B, 20% for Region C and 30% for Region D. Region A has the most passers, but the rate depends on the number of examinees as well."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-08","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Budget of a household with a monthly income of PHP 30,000","headers":["Item","Share of income"],"rows":[["Food","40%"],["Rent","25%"],["Transport","15%"],["Utilities","10%"],["Savings","10%"]]},"question":"How much more does the household spend each month on food than on transport?","options":["PHP 4,500","PHP 6,000","PHP 7,500","PHP 12,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Food takes 25 percentage points more than transport, and 25% of PHP 30,000 is PHP 7,500. PHP 12,000 is the food amount alone and PHP 4,500 is the transport amount alone."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-09","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Attendance at four quarterly seminars","headers":["Quarter","Invited","Attended"],"rows":[["First","80","64"],["Second","120","90"],["Third","100","85"],["Fourth","60","45"]]},"question":"Over the four seminars, how many invitations in total were not used?","options":["66","76","86","96"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total invited is 360 and total attended is 284, and 360 minus 284 is 76. Checking by quarter gives the same result: 16, 30, 15 and 15 add up to 76."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-10","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"table":{"caption":"Scoring of a job applicant","headers":["Criterion","Weight","Score"],"rows":[["Written exam","50%","80"],["Interview","30%","90"],["Experience","20%","70"]]},"question":"What is the applicant's weighted score?","options":["79","80","82","81"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Multiply each score by its weight and add: 40 plus 27 plus 14 is 81. The value 80 is the trap of taking a simple average and ignoring the weights."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-11","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"table":{"caption":"Employees holding civil service eligibility, by division","headers":["Division","Employees","With eligibility"],"rows":[["Administration","40","75%"],["Finance","25","80%"],["Planning","20","60%"],["Legal","15","100%"]]},"question":"What percent of all employees in the four divisions hold eligibility?","options":["77%","78.75%","75%","80%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The eligible counts are 30, 20, 12 and 15, which total 77 out of 100 employees, or 77%. The value 78.75% is the trap of averaging the four percentages without regard to the size of each division."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-12","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"table":{"caption":"Population of a town","headers":["Year","Population"],"rows":[["2020","40,000"],["2022","44,000"],["2024","48,400"]]},"question":"If the same percentage growth per two-year period continues, what will the population be in 2026?","options":["52,800","52,840","53,240","53,400"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The population grows by 10% every two years, and 48,400 plus 10% of 48,400 is 53,240. The value 52,800 is the trap of adding the earlier increase of 4,400 instead of applying the percentage."},{"id":"an-data-interpretation-13","subject":"analytical","skill":"data-interpretation","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"table":{"caption":"Fuel use of agency vehicles on field trips","headers":["Vehicle","Distance (km)","Fuel used (L)"],"rows":[["Van","360","30"],["Pickup","300","30"],["Sedan","420","28"],["Truck","480","80"]]},"question":"How many kilometers per liter does the most fuel-efficient vehicle get?","options":["12 km/L","14 km/L","10 km/L","15 km/L"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dividing distance by fuel gives 12 for the van, 10 for the pickup, 15 for the sedan and 6 for the truck, so the best figure is 15 km/L. The truck covered the longest distance but is the least efficient."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-01","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Evaluate: 48 ÷ 6 + 3 × 5","options":["23","55","39","27"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Divide and multiply before adding: 48 ÷ 6 = 8 and 3 × 5 = 15, so 8 + 15 = 23. Working strictly left to right gives the trap 55, since (8 + 3) × 5 = 55."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-02","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What is 15% of 840?","options":["84","126","112","134"],"answer":1,"explanation":"10% of 840 is 84 and 5% is half of that, 42, so 15% is 84 + 42 = 126. The value 84 is only the 10% step."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-03","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Find the sum: 3/4 + 2/3","options":["5/7","1 1/6","1 5/12","1 7/12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Use the common denominator 12: 9/12 + 8/12 = 17/12 = 1 5/12. Adding tops and bottoms directly gives the trap 5/7, which is smaller than 3/4 and so cannot be the sum."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-04","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Subtract: 7.2 - 3.85","options":["4.35","3.45","4.65","3.35"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Line up the decimal points by writing 7.2 as 7.20: 7.20 - 3.85 = 3.35. The value 4.35 comes from forgetting the borrow from the ones place."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-05","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Evaluate: -12 + 7 - (-4)","options":["-1","-9","1","-15"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Subtracting a negative means adding: -12 + 7 = -5, then -5 + 4 = -1. Treating - (-4) as subtracting 4 gives the trap -9."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-06","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"What is 5/6 of 3/5 of 720?","options":["300","360","432","400"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multiply the fractions first: 5/6 × 3/5 = 1/2, and half of 720 is 360. The value 432 is 3/5 of 720, which stops one step short."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-07","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Evaluate: 18 - 3 × (6 - 4)²","options":["60","-18","6","12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parentheses first gives 2, the exponent gives 4, then 3 × 4 = 12, and 18 - 12 = 6. Subtracting before multiplying gives the trap 60, since 15 × 4 = 60."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-08","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Evaluate: 0.25 × 1.6 ÷ 0.08","options":["0.5","50","20","5"],"answer":3,"explanation":"First 0.25 × 1.6 = 0.4, then 0.4 ÷ 0.08 = 40 ÷ 8 = 5. Shifting the decimal point incorrectly gives the traps 0.5 and 50."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-09","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Divide: 2 1/2 ÷ 5/6","options":["3","2 1/12","1/3","4 1/6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Change to an improper fraction and multiply by the reciprocal: 5/2 × 6/5 = 30/10 = 3. Forgetting to flip the divisor gives 5/2 × 5/6 = 25/12, the trap 2 1/12."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-10","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"36 is what percent of 48?","options":["64%","75%","72%","80%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part over whole: 36/48 = 3/4 = 75%. Dividing both 36 and 48 by 12 keeps the working easy without a calculator."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-11","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Which of these values is the largest?","options":["0.6","62%","5/8","7/12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Convert all to decimals: 0.6 = 0.600, 62% = 0.620, 5/8 = 0.625 and 7/12 is about 0.583, so 5/8 is the largest. It beats 62% by only 0.005, so cutting 5/8 off at 0.62 makes the two look tied, which is the trap."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-12","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"The number 240 is increased by 25%, and the result is then decreased by 20%. What is the final value?","options":["228","252","288","240"],"answer":3,"explanation":"240 × 1.25 = 300, then 300 × 0.80 = 240, so the value returns to where it began. Netting 25% and 20% to a 5% rise gives the trap 252, but the two percents apply to different bases."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-13","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Evaluate: -8 × 3 + (-20) ÷ (-4)","options":["-19","-29","19","-1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"-8 × 3 = -24 and (-20) ÷ (-4) = +5, so -24 + 5 = -19. Giving the quotient a negative sign leads to the trap -29, but a negative divided by a negative is positive."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-14","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Evaluate: (3/8 - 1/4) × 16","options":["4","2","10","1/2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inside the parentheses, 3/8 - 2/8 = 1/8, and 1/8 × 16 = 2. Adding instead of subtracting gives 5/8 × 16 = 10, which is the trap."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-15","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Evaluate: 3 1/4 - 1 5/6 + 2/3","options":["3/4","1 11/12","2 1/12","2 1/4"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Over 12: 39/12 - 22/12 + 8/12 = 25/12 = 2 1/12. Subtracting the 2/3 as well gives the trap 3/4, but only the 1 5/6 is being taken away; the 2/3 is added."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-16","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Evaluate: [24 - 4 × (7 - 2)] ÷ 2 + 3²","options":["59","14","7","11"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Inner parentheses give 5, then 4 × 5 = 20 and 24 - 20 = 4, so 4 ÷ 2 = 2 and 2 + 9 = 11. Subtracting 24 - 4 before multiplying gives 20 × 5 ÷ 2 + 9 = 59, which is the trap."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-17","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"A number decreased by 30% equals 420. What is the number?","options":["600","546","588","640"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The result is 70% of the number, so the number is 420 ÷ 0.70 = 600. Adding 30% to 420 gives the trap 546, but the 30% is taken from the original number, not from 420."},{"id":"nu-basic-operations-18","subject":"numerical","skill":"basic-operations","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Evaluate: 0.125 + 3/8 + (12.5% of 4)","options":["0.75","1","1.5","0.625"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Here 3/8 = 0.375 and 12.5% of 4 = 0.125 × 4 = 0.5, so 0.125 + 0.375 + 0.5 = 1. Adding 12.5% itself instead of 12.5% of 4 gives the trap 0.625."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-01","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What number comes next? 4, 9, 14, 19, 24, ...","options":["28","34","29","30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each term is 5 more than the one before: 24 + 5 = 29."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-02","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What comes next? 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, 4/5, ...","options":["5/6","6/7","5/7","4/6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The numerator and the denominator each go up by 1 from term to term, so 4/5 is followed by 5/6."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-03","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What number comes next? 5, 10, 20, 40, 80, ...","options":["120","160","150","140"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each term is double the one before: 80 × 2 = 160. Adding the last gap of 40 again gives the trap 120, but the gaps themselves are doubling."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-04","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What number comes next? -17, -11, -5, 1, 7, ...","options":["12","15","-13","13"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The series adds 6 each time, passing through zero without changing the rule: -17 + 6 = -11, -11 + 6 = -5, -5 + 6 = 1, 1 + 6 = 7, and 7 + 6 = 13."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-05","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"What number comes next? 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ...","options":["30","35","49","36"],"answer":3,"explanation":"These are the perfect squares 1², 2², 3², 4², 5², so the next is 6² = 36. The gaps 3, 5, 7, 9 confirm it, since 25 + 11 = 36."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-06","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 2, 5, 11, 23, 47, ...","options":["95","94","71","96"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each term is double the previous term plus 1: 47 × 2 + 1 = 95. The gaps 3, 6, 12, 24 double as well, so the next gap is 48 and 47 + 48 = 95."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-07","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 3, 4, 7, 8, 11, 12, ...","options":["13","15","14","16"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The steps alternate +1, +3, +1, +3, +1. The last step into 12 was +1, so the next is +3: 12 + 3 = 15. Applying +1 twice in a row gives the trap 13."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-08","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ...","options":["18","20","21","26"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each term is the sum of the two terms before it: 5 + 8 = 13, so the next is 8 + 13 = 21."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-09","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 7, 9, 13, 19, 27, ...","options":["35","36","39","37"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The gaps are 2, 4, 6, 8, rising by 2 each time, so the next gap is 10 and 27 + 10 = 37. Repeating the gap of 8 gives the trap 35."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-10","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 0.5, 1.25, 2, 2.75, 3.5, ...","options":["4","4.25","4.5","5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each term is 0.75 more than the one before: 0.5 + 0.75 = 1.25, 1.25 + 0.75 = 2, and so on, so 3.5 + 0.75 = 4.25."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-11","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 100, 91, 83, 76, 70, ...","options":["65","64","66","63"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The amounts subtracted shrink by 1 each time: 9, 8, 7, 6, so the next is 5 and 70 - 5 = 65. Subtracting 6 again gives the trap 64."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-12","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, ...","options":["196","256","216","343"],"answer":2,"explanation":"These are the perfect cubes 1³, 2³, 3³, 4³, 5³, so the next is 6³ = 216. The value 343 is 7³, which skips a term."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-13","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 10, 3, 12, 6, 14, 9, ...","options":["12","15","18","16"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two series alternate: the 1st, 3rd and 5th terms go 10, 12, 14 (+2) and the 2nd, 4th and 6th go 3, 6, 9 (+3). The next is the 7th term, so 14 + 2 = 16; the value 12 is what would follow 9, one place later."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-14","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Find the next term: 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, ...","options":["240","600","720","840"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The multiplier rises by 1 each step: ×2, ×3, ×4, ×5, so the next is ×6 and 120 × 6 = 720. Repeating ×5 gives the trap 600."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-15","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Find the next term: 4, 6, 12, 14, 28, 30, ...","options":["32","60","58","62"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The operations alternate +2 and ×2: 4 + 2 = 6, 6 × 2 = 12, 12 + 2 = 14, 14 × 2 = 28, 28 + 2 = 30. The next operation is ×2, so 30 × 2 = 60; the value 32 uses +2 twice in a row."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-16","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Find the next term: 2, 3, 6, 18, 108, ...","options":["1944","648","1296","2160"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each term is the product of the two before it: 2 × 3 = 6, 3 × 6 = 18, 6 × 18 = 108, so the next is 18 × 108 = 1944."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-17","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Find the next term: 1, 4, 3, 16, 5, 36, ...","options":["8","49","64","7"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two series alternate: the odd places hold the odd numbers 1, 3, 5, and the even places hold the squares 2², 4², 6². The next is an odd place, so it is 7; the value 64 is 8², which would come one place later."},{"id":"nu-number-sequence-18","subject":"numerical","skill":"number-sequence","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Find the next term: 5, 6, 14, 41, 105, ...","options":["216","169","230","241"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The gaps are 1, 8, 27, 64, which are the cubes 1³, 2³, 3³, 4³. The next gap is 5³ = 125, so 105 + 125 = 230."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-01","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"A sari-sari store owner buys a case of soft drinks for ₱480 and sells it at a 25% markup. What is the selling price of the case?","options":["₱600","₱505","₱580","₱720"],"answer":0,"explanation":"25% of ₱480 is ₱120, so the selling price is ₱480 + ₱120 = ₱600. The figure ₱505 wrongly adds ₱25 instead of 25%."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-02","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Marites and Jun share ₱3,600 in the ratio 5:4, with Marites getting the larger share. How much does Marites receive?","options":["₱1,600","₱2,000","₱2,400","₱1,800"],"answer":1,"explanation":"There are 5 + 4 = 9 parts, so one part is ₱3,600 ÷ 9 = ₱400 and Marites gets 5 × ₱400 = ₱2,000. The figure ₱1,600 is Jun's share."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-03","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"A provincial bus leaving Cubao averages 60 km/h for 4.5 hours. How far does it travel in that time?","options":["240 km","255 km","270 km","285 km"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Distance is rate times time: 60 × 4.5 = 270 km. Handling the half hour separately helps: 60 × 4 = 240, plus 30 for the half hour."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-04","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"A government clerk with a monthly basic salary of ₱30,000 pays a 9% GSIS contribution. How much is deducted each month?","options":["₱2,100","₱2,400","₱3,300","₱2,700"],"answer":3,"explanation":"1% of ₱30,000 is ₱300, so 9% is 9 × ₱300 = ₱2,700."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-05","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":1,"question":"Mang Tonyo is 3 times as old as his son. If his son is 14 now, how old will Mang Tonyo be in 5 years?","options":["47 years old","42 years old","57 years old","45 years old"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mang Tonyo is now 3 × 14 = 42, so in 5 years he will be 42 + 5 = 47. The trap 57 triples the son's future age, but the 3-to-1 relationship holds only for their present ages."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-06","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A farmer in Nueva Ecija can harvest a rice field alone in 6 days, and his helper can do it alone in 12 days. Working together, how many days will the harvest take?","options":["9 days","4 days","3 days","5 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Their combined daily rate is 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 = 1/4 of the field, so the harvest takes 4 days. Averaging 6 and 12 gives the trap 9 days, yet two people together must finish faster than the quicker one alone."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-07","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A Batangas coffee shop blends 3 kg of barako beans costing ₱500 per kg with 2 kg of arabica beans costing ₱750 per kg. What is the cost per kilogram of the blend?","options":["₱625","₱575","₱600","₱650"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total cost is 3 × ₱500 + 2 × ₱750 = ₱3,000 for 5 kg, so ₱3,000 ÷ 5 = ₱600 per kg. The simple average ₱625 ignores that there is more of the cheaper bean."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-08","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A polo barong marked ₱1,250 is sold at 20% off, and a further 10% is taken off the sale price. How much does the buyer pay?","options":["₱875","₱950","₱1,000","₱900"],"answer":3,"explanation":"₱1,250 × 0.80 = ₱1,000, then ₱1,000 × 0.90 = ₱900. Adding the discounts to 30% gives the trap ₱875, but the second discount applies to the already reduced price."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-09","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"At 7:00 AM, two buses leave two towns in Mindanao that are 240 km apart and travel toward each other on the same highway, one at 50 km/h and the other at 70 km/h. At what time do they meet?","options":["9:00 AM","8:30 AM","9:30 AM","10:00 AM"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moving toward each other, they close the gap at 50 + 70 = 120 km/h, so they meet after 240 ÷ 120 = 2 hours, at 9:00 AM."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-10","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A carinderia recipe uses 450 g of pork for 6 servings of adobo. At the same rate, how much pork is needed for 20 servings?","options":["1.2 kg","1.5 kg","1.35 kg","1.8 kg"],"answer":1,"explanation":"One serving uses 450 ÷ 6 = 75 g, so 20 servings use 75 × 20 = 1,500 g, which is 1.5 kg."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-11","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A household's monthly electric bill rose from ₱2,400 to ₱3,000. What is the percent increase?","options":["20%","30%","25%","60%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The increase is ₱600, and it is compared with the original bill: 600 ÷ 2,400 = 1/4 = 25%. Dividing by the new bill of ₱3,000 gives the trap 20%."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-12","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Rosa is 8 years older than Carlo. In 4 years, Rosa will be twice as old as Carlo. How old is Carlo now?","options":["6 years old","8 years old","12 years old","4 years old"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Let c be Carlo's present age: c + 8 + 4 = 2(c + 4), so c + 12 = 2c + 8 and c = 4. Check: now 4 and 12, in 4 years 8 and 16, and 16 is twice 8. The value 12 is Rosa's present age."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-13","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"A teacher borrows ₱25,000 from a cooperative at 6% simple interest per year. How much interest is due after 18 months?","options":["₱2,250","₱1,500","₱2,500","₱2,700"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interest is principal × rate × time with time in years: 25,000 × 0.06 × 1.5 = ₱2,250. The figure ₱1,500 covers only the first 12 months."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-14","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":2,"question":"Eight workers can repaint a barangay hall in 15 days. Working at the same rate, how many days will 12 workers need?","options":["12 days","10 days","11 days","9 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The job equals 8 × 15 = 120 worker-days, so 12 workers need 120 ÷ 12 = 10 days. More workers means fewer days, so this is an inverse proportion, not a direct one."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-15","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"A motorcycle rider in Davao covers the first 40 km of a trip at 40 km/h and the next 60 km in slower traffic at 20 km/h. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","options":["30 km/h","24 km/h","25 km/h","28 km/h"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Average speed is total distance over total time. The times are 40 ÷ 40 = 1 hour and 60 ÷ 20 = 3 hours, so 100 km ÷ 4 hours = 25 km/h. Averaging the two speeds gives the trap 30 km/h, which ignores the longer time spent at the slower speed."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-16","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"A barangay sold 150 tickets to its fiesta variety show, adult tickets at ₱50 each and child tickets at ₱20 each, and collected ₱5,400. How many adult tickets were sold?","options":["70 tickets","90 tickets","100 tickets","80 tickets"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Let a be the number of adult tickets: 50a + 20(150 - a) = 5,400, so 30a + 3,000 = 5,400 and a = 80. Check: 80 × ₱50 + 70 × ₱20 = ₱4,000 + ₱1,400 = ₱5,400. The value 70 is the child ticket count."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-17","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"A rural health unit has 20 liters of a 15% disinfectant solution. How many liters of water must be added to dilute it to 12%?","options":["5 L","3 L","4 L","6 L"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The solution holds 20 × 0.15 = 3 L of disinfectant, and adding water does not change that. For 3 L to be 12% the total must be 3 ÷ 0.12 = 25 L, so add 25 - 20 = 5 L. The value 3 L is the disinfectant, not the water to add."},{"id":"nu-word-problems-18","subject":"numerical","skill":"word-problems","lang":"en","difficulty":3,"question":"Nena can paint a school fence alone in 10 hours and Dodong can paint it alone in 15 hours. They work together for 3 hours, then Nena leaves. How many more hours does Dodong need to finish the fence alone?","options":["6 hours","7.5 hours","9 hours","12 hours"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Together they paint 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6 of the fence per hour, so 3 hours finishes 1/2 of it. Dodong paints the other 1/2 at 1/15 per hour, which takes 1/2 ÷ 1/15 = 7.5 hours."}];
